WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.119
When Claude calls a tool, it generates arguments based entirely on your schema.

2
00:00:05.119 --> 00:00:08.159
If the schema is vague, Claude's arguments will be too.

3
00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:14.640
A well-designed schema acts like a contract, specifying what to send and in what format.

4
00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:19.440
For this video, we'll cover how JSON Schema drives tool input design

5
00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:23.280
and why precision is the biggest lever for call reliability.

6
00:00:24.319 --> 00:00:28.319
Every argument Claude generates is shaped by what your schema declares,

7
00:00:28.319 --> 00:00:32.080
so it's worth knowing exactly what that object contains.

8
00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.959
A tool schema is the JSON schema object you attach to your tool definition.

9
00:00:36.959 --> 00:00:43.759
It specifies every accepted parameter, its type, any constraints, and which fields are required.

10
00:00:43.759 --> 00:00:47.520
Claude uses this schema to shape the arguments it generates.

11
00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.159
By default, the schema is best effort guidance.

12
00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:56.560
To guarantee arguments conform exactly, you set strict tool use on the definition.

13
00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.639
A tool input parameter is any named field inside that schema,

14
00:01:00.639 --> 00:01:03.200
carrying a value Claude has to supply.

15
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:06.720
Parameters can be required or optional, typed, and described.

16
00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:12.419
Schema validation is the step where generated arguments are checked against the declared structure.

17
00:01:13.599 --> 00:01:19.199
JSON Schema is the standard format for declaring tool inputs in the Anthropic API.

18
00:01:19.199 --> 00:01:23.360
It's a machine-readable object that tells Claude which fields exist,

19
00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:26.559
what types they accept, and what constraints apply.

20
00:01:26.559 --> 00:01:28.080
Here's an important detail.

21
00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.639
Claude reads your schema at inference time, not just at definition time.

22
00:01:32.639 --> 00:01:36.160
That means every property you declare is actively shaping

23
00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:39.120
how Claude constructs its arguments right now in this call.

24
00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:45.120
If you enable strict tool use, the model is held to the grammar-enforced parts of your schema,

25
00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:50.720
its types, enums, and required fields, which acts as a safety net at generation time.

26
00:01:51.279 --> 00:01:55.040
Length and range bounds stay advisory, so that safety net

27
00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:58.800
only helps where your schema uses the constraints that are actually enforced.

28
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.720
Parameter names are the first signal Claude uses to understand what a field is for.

29
00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:10.720
A field called ID could mean a user ID, an order ID, a product ID, or a session ID,

30
00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:12.399
depending on context.

31
00:02:12.399 --> 00:02:16.559
A field called customer ID removes that ambiguity completely.

32
00:02:16.559 --> 00:02:18.479
The guidance here is straightforward.

33
00:02:18.479 --> 00:02:24.880
Use specific descriptive names in SnakeCase, avoid abbreviations the model might misinterpret,

34
00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:29.039
and make sure the name reflects the domain meaning of the field

35
00:02:29.039 --> 00:02:31.839
rather than how you've implemented it internally.

36
00:02:31.839 --> 00:02:37.199
This matters because Claude is reading names as semantic signals, not just identifiers.

37
00:02:37.199 --> 00:02:41.919
When parameter names are clear, Claude is far less likely to populate them

38
00:02:41.919 --> 00:02:43.919
with plausible but wrong values.

39
00:02:44.479 --> 00:02:48.000
Naming anti-patterns are worth calling out directly,

40
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.720
because they show up all the time in real schemas.

41
00:02:51.199 --> 00:02:58.559
Generic names like data, input, or value give Claude no context about the field's purpose,

42
00:02:58.559 --> 00:03:01.759
so it's left to infer from surrounding conversation.

43
00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:06.720
Overloaded names, where the same field name means different things in different tools,

44
00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:11.600
compound the problem because Claude builds up associations over a session

45
00:03:12.399 --> 00:03:14.160
that carry over incorrectly.

46
00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:21.679
Cryptic abbreviations like usractnum or acttpcd force the model to guess at meaning,

47
00:03:21.679 --> 00:03:24.160
and increase the risk that it guesses wrong.

48
00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:29.039
Each of these patterns produces tool calls that look structurally valid,

49
00:03:29.039 --> 00:03:30.800
but carry incorrect content.

50
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.600
Here's a design insight that surprises a lot of developers.

51
00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:39.600
Parameter descriptions aren't just documentation for humans.

52
00:03:39.679 --> 00:03:43.360
Claude reads them at inference time as part of argument generation.

53
00:03:43.919 --> 00:03:49.520
A description like the user id technically communicates what kind of data belongs in the field,

54
00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:53.440
but a richly specified description tells Claude the source,

55
00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:56.800
the expected format, and the constraints all at once.

56
00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:59.600
The strongest descriptions name the field's purpose,

57
00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:03.520
the valid values it accepts, and how it should behave at the edges.

58
00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:08.720
Omitting descriptions degrades argument quality even when your types are correct.

59
00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:11.520
Think of every description as a mini instruction,

60
00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:14.720
written directly to the model, because that's exactly what it is.

61
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.239
Let's make the contrast concrete.

62
00:04:18.239 --> 00:04:21.200
A weak description says the user id.

63
00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:24.640
That's technically accurate, but seriously underspecified.

64
00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:28.480
Claude knows it's some kind of identifier, but it doesn't know the format,

65
00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:33.519
where it comes from, whether it's numeric, or a uuid, or how long it should be.

66
00:04:33.519 --> 00:04:38.799
A strong description says uuid of the authenticated user from the session token,

67
00:04:38.799 --> 00:04:46.239
formatted as 32 hexadecimal digits, displayed in 5 hyphen separated groups, 36 characters total.

68
00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:53.200
That tells Claude the exact format, the source of the value, and the length, all in one sentence.

69
00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:55.359
There's no room for misinterpretation.

70
00:04:55.359 --> 00:04:59.839
The difference in argument quality between these two descriptions is measurable,

71
00:04:59.839 --> 00:05:05.359
especially for identifier fields where the format has to be precisely right, or the call fails.

72
00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:11.519
Under strict tool use, JSON schema constraints aren't all enforced the same way.

73
00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:17.119
Type, enum, and supported format values are grammar-enforced,

74
00:05:17.119 --> 00:05:19.440
so Claude can't generate anything outside them.

75
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:25.440
The type property declares whether a field is a string, an integer, a number, a boolean,

76
00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:32.079
or an array. The format property adds a semantic layer, hints like date, time, or email,

77
00:05:32.079 --> 00:05:35.920
that tell both Claude and your validators what the string represents.

78
00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:40.000
Pattern is a regular expression the value has to match,

79
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:43.119
and it's only partially enforced, so check it in your own code.

80
00:05:43.679 --> 00:05:48.799
Length and range, bounds like min-length and maximum, are purely advisory,

81
00:05:48.799 --> 00:05:54.079
validated after the fact. Lean on the enforced constraints for hard guarantees,

82
00:05:54.079 --> 00:05:58.320
and re-validate the advisory ones before the call reaches your backend.

83
00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:04.799
There's a line worth drawing between the parts of a schema that constrain Claude,

84
00:06:04.799 --> 00:06:06.640
and the parts that only persuade it.

85
00:06:07.359 --> 00:06:10.559
Types and enums are enforced at generation.

86
00:06:11.119 --> 00:06:15.600
Names and descriptions steer, and length bounds get checked after the fact.

87
00:06:16.239 --> 00:06:20.959
Once you know which is which, you stop trusting a good description to guarantee everything.

88
00:06:21.519 --> 00:06:23.920
The rest you validate in your own code.

89
00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:27.760
That's a wrap on this one. I'll see you in the next video.

